.TH QARGI 1 "" "qargi 0.2.0"
.SH NAME
qargi \- A gathering place for agent work: dispatch, review, remember
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qargi
.I command
[\fIverb\fR] [\fIarguments\fR]
.br
.B qargi help
[\fIcommand\fR|\fItopic\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
qargi dispatches one\-off, task\-scoped development tasks to sandboxed agents and takes the work back as reviewable patches. An agent is a member on this machine (local, the default) or a cloud agent (only when you ask for it in that request). Agents work in their own clone and never push. A team adds a lead that coordinates members for build, write and review on one task. Everything qargi stores about you and your projects lives outside every repository. Inside a project's repository you can leave out its name.
.PP
Every command takes \fB\-\-help\fR. A command that changes shared resources prints a plan and applies it only with \fB\-\-plan\fR and \fB\-\-approved\fR; a cloud command needs \fB\-\-asked\fR. See APPROVALS.
.SH CONCEPTS
Task: you describe one task and an executable check that decides it is done. An agent works on it in its own clone and hands back numbered patches, one per commit, with a manifest. Nothing is pushed.
.PP
Roles are the four abilities and the lead: build changes code, write writes the operational docs, review judges a change and changes no file, test writes and runs tests, lead coordinates a team. Each ability is defined by its role text, which comes with the task block. The local workers are members you name and give abilities (qargi agent add NAME \-\-roles build,test); qargi agent for ABILITY prints the member that serves it. A role has its own model, effort, skills and packages, stored in the project's answers.
.PP
Local or cloud: local (a member on this machine) is the default. The cloud is used only when your request for that task names it, and every cloud verb records your words with \-\-asked.
.PP
You decide: the coordinator re\-verifies everything an agent returns (qargi review check), applies it as separate commits, and asks you before anything is pushed.
.SH APPROVALS
A command that changes something shared prints a plan first. The plan ends with a plan token, a hash of what would be done.
.PP
To apply, run the same command with \-\-plan TOKEN \-\-approved "your words". If the plan changed in between, the token no longer matches and nothing happens.
.PP
\-\-asked "your request" is required by every cloud verb: it records that you asked for the cloud. Both are written to an append\-only audit log in the state directory.
.PP
qargi lead grant and revoke are interactive instead: you confirm each step at the terminal, and that is the approval.
.PP
The rules. The token covers the plan the person saw, and an apply refuses when the plan is no longer that one. The \-\-approved text is recorded, not verified: it is not proof that a person agreed. Never allow\-list a gated verb in the runtime's settings: the permission prompt is the person's real control. A member never applies: it reports the plan and its token, and the lead shows them to the person (what the member's guard refuses: qargi help guard).
.SH GUARD
qargi local verify is the backstop of the guard, a hook on a local member's calls: a barrier, not a wall.
.PP
It refuses verbs that change shared state: skill and tool new, import, seal, remove, unlink, link, enable, disable, accept and unaccept; discover install; import; packs apply, seed, link and unlink; project set (models answers), init, new, link and unlink; provider new, import, seal, remove, enable, disable, attest, accept, check and adapter\-run (the rest read); release publish; marshal claim, set, renew, release, take, takeover, finish, enable, disable, key, signers and resign (handoff only with \-\-write); feature start, finish and verify; memory init, move, split, merge and copy; cloud set, unset and login; upgrade; local run, prepare, verify and clean; agent remove; team run; task.
.PP
It refuses writes in the pack library, credentials, the install record, the runtime's skills, agents and plugin directories, the installed copy, the state's render, providers, marshal and feature directories, its ref journal, the memory tiers, ~/.config/anthropic, and the lead's git configuration and session files (~/.gitconfig, ~/.claude/settings.json). Reading stays allowed except the lead's signing key: copying, linking or archiving its directory, or searching it, is refused; copying into the home directory is not. It refuses git push, git remote changes, git \-c alias.*, gh, ssh, scp, sftp and rsync to a host.
.PP
A command is cut at every ; && || | and newline, inside quotes and heredocs too, and a substitution is a command: no refused verb, push or tag in text on the command line. Commit with git commit \-F FILE. A cd it cannot read refuses relative writes.
.PP
The read\-only class (review) writes only OUT and TMP: every change to the clone is refused, git included, and a write through a name the guard cannot read. Nobody copies, extracts or links into the run directory.
.PP
Setting QARGI_NESTED, which skips a plan token check, or setting or unsetting QARGI_LEAD, is refused.
.PP
A refusal is a result: report it; do not work around it.
.SH NAMING
Project and role are single lower\-case tokens. An instance is <project>\-<role>, for example acme\-build. A session is <instance>\-<task\-slug>, and the agent's branch is agent/<task\-slug>.
.PP
Cloud resources follow <project>\-<component>[\-<role>]: the agent <project>\-<role> (acme\-build), the deployment <project>\-deployment\-<role>, the shared <project>\-environment, and the stores <project>\-memory and <project>\-reference. The lead is <project>\-lead, and so is its agent.
.PP
qargi instances normalize renames existing resources to match.
.SH MEMORY
<project>\-memory is where agents write verified how\-to facts they learn, under their own role folder (shared/ is curated and reached by promotion). <project>\-reference is read\-only: rules, workflow and conventions you and the coordinator curate. Both have shared/ for every role and roles/<role>/ for one.
.PP
An agent reads each store's README.md, then shared/INDEX.md and its role's index, then only the notes its task needs. The canon (qargi canon) fixes the layout and the note format. Read one note with qargi memory get.
.PP
qargi memory split, merge and copy move notes between two stores (directories, .jsonl dumps or store ids). Nothing is deleted: what leaves a store is archived under /_archive/<date>/, a collision goes to /_conflicts/<date>/, and the origin of every note written is added to its Sources: line.
.SH PUSH
Only the lead ever pushes, never a member, and only as the project's lead.push grant says. The default is nothing.
.PP
This Claude Code session as lead: lead.push.local lets it push agent/* branches and open pull requests without asking each time.
.PP
A cloud lead, or one started in the console: lead.push.cloud = branches. It has a GitHub tool with a fixed allowlist and the merge tool asked of you. Set it up with qargi lead grant. A merge or a release always needs your explicit approval.
.PP
The GitHub tool cannot create releases; those stay with this session.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Set up
.TP
.B qargi project
Store the answers about a project, once, outside the repository.
.PP
The answers are the repository, its long\-lived branches, the roles that exist (build, write, review, test) with each role's base branch, model, effort, packages and skills, the memory stores, the budget, and optionally a lead with teams and a push grant. Provisioning and dispatch read them. Push policy per role is none: an agent never pushes; the lead's grant is separate (qargi help push).
.RS
.TP
.BI "template " "NAME"
Print an answers file with defaults.
.br
Edit it, then store it with init. models.provider is empty and must be set (the provider the project's agents run on; there is no default); project validate reports it while it is not. No role names a model: it takes the provider's tier once one is set.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "init " "NAME"
Validate and store an answers file.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-answers " "FILE"
the answers file to store.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-force
replace stored answers.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "new " "NAME"
Start a new project: its directory, a git repository on the methodology's branches and generic files.
.br
Prints the plan (directory, branches, files, deployment options with instructions) and a token; with \-\-plan and \-\-approved it creates the directory, runs git init, writes the generic files in a first commit and creates the long\-lived branches. Nothing is pushed and no remote is created: say where it goes with git. The files mention no tool of yours.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-methodology " "NAME"
the methodology (default gitflow).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-dir " "DIR"
where to create it (default ./NAME; must be absent or empty).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-param " "NAME=VALUE"
a methodology parameter as name=value (repeat), for example test_command.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-url " "URL"
the repository URL; with it the qargi answers are stored too.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-deployment " "ID"
a deployment option to record and explain (see qargi methodology deployment).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider " "NAME"
the provider the project's agents run on (models.provider, with its models); not stored when omitted (qargi provider list).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider\-local " "NAME"
the runtime of the local surface (models.provider.local); use it with \-\-provider\-cloud instead of \-\-provider.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider\-cloud " "NAME"
the runtime of the cloud surface (models.provider.cloud).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "attach " "[DIR]"
Attach to an existing repository: read it, show the answers and the gaps, store on approval.
.br
Reads the repository (branches, tags, version and changelog files, test command, CI) and changes nothing in it. Prints the answers it would store, the gaps against the methodology and what it could not determine, and a plan token. With \-\-plan and \-\-approved it stores the answers outside the repository. The safe gaps are closed by qargi project adopt, the rest by hand. It reads the tree of the methodology's integration branch (develop), else of the default branch, else of HEAD, and says which; never the working tree. It prints origin as read and the https url it normalises to. If answers are already stored it shows stored against detected, lists the fields the stored file lacks, stays read\-only and gives no plan token; with \-\-force the plan replaces what it detects and keeps what it cannot (the roles' models and effort, cloud, memory, budget). It never guesses a provider: models.provider (and the roles' models) are stored only with \-\-provider NAME, and without it the output says so; with \-\-force \-\-provider fills in a provider that is not set and never replaces one.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-name " "NAME"
the project name (default: from the remote or the directory).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-methodology " "NAME"
the methodology to compare with (default gitflow).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-url " "URL"
the repository URL, when it has no origin remote.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider " "NAME"
the provider the project's agents run on (models.provider, with its models); not stored when omitted (qargi provider list).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider\-local " "NAME"
the runtime of the local surface (models.provider.local); use it with \-\-provider\-cloud instead of \-\-provider.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider\-cloud " "NAME"
the runtime of the cloud surface (models.provider.cloud).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-force
replace the detected answers, keep the rest.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "adopt " "[NAME]"
Adopt an attached repository: create the missing long\-lived branches and the missing methodology files on a branch to review.
.br
Prints the plan and a token: the local branches it would create (at the default branch tip; main stands for master; none is moved) and the files from the methodology that do not exist, written as one commit on agent/adopt\-NAME cut from develop. With \-\-plan and \-\-approved it creates exactly that. Existing branches and files are never changed, nothing is pushed or checked out, and a second run finds nothing to do. Changelog format, tag format and CI content are listed as left for you. Refused (exit 2) with a dirty working tree, a detached HEAD, no commits, a project that is not attached, and in a member run.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-methodology " "NAME"
the methodology (default: the project's, else gitflow).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "set " "[NAME] PATH VALUE"
Change one answer.
.br
PATH is dotted (budget_cents, roles.write.effort, lead.push); VALUE is JSON or a plain string. models.extensions and models.accept_no_barrier are refused here: they change only through qargi provider enable|disable and qargi provider accept.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "show " "[NAME]"
Print the stored answers and what they mean.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the stored projects.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "validate " "FILE"
Check an answers file without storing it.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "link " "[PROJECT]"
Make this work tree load the project's skills, agents and memory from ~/.qargi.
.br
One plan and one token for every resource, each placed and linked the way the project's runtime says (a directory link, one link per entry, or a copy). A real directory in the way is moved to NAME.qargi\-backup, never deleted. Run it inside a work tree to link that one; nothing records which work trees exist. A member's guard refuses it.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-resource " "LIST"
what to link: skills, agents, memory (comma separated; default every one the runtime links and that exists).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "unlink " "[PROJECT]"
Undo project link in this work tree and restore the backups.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-resource " "LIST"
what to link: skills, agents, memory (comma separated; default every one the runtime links and that exists).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi methodology
List and inspect the development and release methodologies.
.PP
A methodology is the way a project is developed and released, as data: its branches, versioning and changelog, test tiers, CI, release steps and deployment options, and the generic files a new project starts with. One ships with qargi (gitflow); add your own under ~/.qargi/config/methodologies/NAME/ (methodology.json and a scaffold/ directory). These verbs only read; qargi project new and adopt write, and only after a plan.
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the methodologies and where they live.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "show " "NAME"
Print a methodology: branches, tests, CI, release steps, parameters, scaffold, deployment options.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "check " "[NAME]"
Validate one methodology, or all of them.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "files " "NAME"
Print the scaffold rendered with the parameters, writing nothing.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-param " "NAME=VALUE"
a parameter as name=value (repeat).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "deployment " "NAME [ID]"
List the deployment options, or print one option's instructions.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi cloud
Which ant profile and workspace each project uses.
.PP
A project can be bound to an ant profile and the workspace that profile must reach (the project's optional cloud answer). Every qargi command about that project then runs ant under that profile, and a cloud call is refused if the profile no longer reaches the workspace, so work never lands in another project's workspace. A project without a binding uses ant's active profile. qargi stores and checks the binding; it never reads or writes a token. login runs ant auth login on your terminal; a member or a script cannot.
.RS
.TP
.B list
Print every project's binding.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "show " "[PROJECT]"
Print one project's binding.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "set " "[PROJECT]"
Bind a project to an ant profile and a workspace.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " "NAME"
the ant profile name.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-workspace\-id " "ID"
the workspace the profile must reach (wrkspc_...).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "unset " "[PROJECT]"
Remove a project's binding.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "check " "[PROJECT]"
Check that the profile exists and reaches the workspace (reads the ant profile only).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "login " "[PROJECT]"
Log the project's profile in, bound to its workspace (your terminal only).
.RE
.TP
.B qargi agent
Name the members: the local agents that do the work.
.PP
A member is a local agent you name, rendered from the one member definition qargi ships, with the roles it serves: build, write, review, test (all four by default). The task block names the role and carries its role text (roles/<role>/ROLE.md); there is no skill to load. Review is read\-only as a rule: the guard refuses changes to the clone under that role. Under every role it refuses the verbs that change a skill (qargi skill|tool new, import, seal, remove, link, unlink, enable, disable; qargi discover install; qargi packs apply) and writes in the pack library, the credentials directory and install.json, ~/.claude/skills and any .claude/skills directory; it fails closed. Dispatch spawns the member qargi agent for ROLE prints.
.RS
.TP
.BI "add " "NAME"
Create a member under a name you choose.
.br
A member belongs to a project, and the definition is rendered by the provider of that project. Refuses a name that exists, a name that is not kebab\-case, the name of a built\-in agent type of that provider, no \-\-project (exit 2), and a project with no provider (exit 2: there is no default provider). Nothing is written to a runtime's user\-level directory.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-roles " "LIST"
comma\-separated roles it serves (default build,write,review,test).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-model " "MODEL"
model alias or id for the definition (default: the project's model for the first role, else the shipped default; see agent models).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-effort " "LEVEL"
effort level, written into the definition and recorded with the member (default as for \-\-model).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project the member belongs to (required): its models.provider renders it, and the file goes where the runtime links project agents (the checkout, hidden from git, or the project render once `project link` is applied).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the members of every project, their roles and definitions.
.br
One row per member, with no header line: PROJECT, NAME, ROLES (comma separated), MODEL/EFFORT, FILE, then STALE, DRIFT or MISSING in brackets when the file or the render is out of date. Members are kept per project, so two projects can each have a worker.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
only the members of this project.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "rename " "OLD NEW"
Rename a member and its definition.
.br
Within its project. The project is \-\-project, else the one in effect, else the only project that has a member OLD; a name two or more projects hold, with none named or in effect, is refused (exit 2) and lists them.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project the member belongs to (default: the one in effect when it has the member, else the only project that has it).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "refresh " "NAME"
Re\-render a member from the member definition qargi ships now.
.br
The project is found as for rename. Keeps its name, roles and model. Use it after an upgrade changes the definition (for example its hook); an unchanged member is left alone. Sessions started afterwards use the new definition.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project the member belongs to (default: the one in effect when it has the member, else the only project that has it).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B models
Show the base members, the profiles with their resolved models and efforts, and each member.
.br
Two base members, one per guard class: worker (build, write, test; may write) and reviewer (review; read\-only). Everything else is a profile {base, tier, effort, purpose} resolved at dispatch: the shipped ones are in lib/model\-defaults.json (build, write, test, review, auditor, advisor, researcher, lead), a project adds or overrides its own under profiles.NAME in its answers, and a provider file (lib/providers/NAME.json; the project's models.provider) maps the tiers fast, balanced, deep and frontier to models. The frontier tier (researcher) is opt\-in. Problems are flagged on their row, never fatal: a member that is not a base (nothing dispatches it), a missing base, a refused project profile, a mixed\-class member, a tier with no alias, a frontier member outside researcher. A project is needed, because its models.provider decides the models: \-\-project, else the one in effect (with none, exit 2). The members shown are that project's (`agent list` shows all projects'). Read\-only.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
machine\-readable.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "remove " "NAME"
Delete a member's definition and its record.
.br
The project is found as for rename: \-\-project, else the one in effect, else the only project that has the member; a name in two or more projects with none named is refused (exit 2).
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project the member belongs to (default: the one in effect when it has the member, else the only project that has it).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "for " "ROLE"
Print how to dispatch a role: the base member, the model alias and the effort.
.br
The base member is the one the profile names (worker for build, write and test, reviewer for review) when the project has registered it; no other member serves, and without it the verb says which base to add. A read\-only role never resolves to a member that can write. Plain output is one line: MEMBER MODEL EFFORT PROFILE; pass MODEL and EFFORT to the Agent tool with the member as subagent_type.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project whose members serve and whose profiles apply (default: the project of the current directory).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " "NAME"
a profile of the role's class (a shipped one such as auditor, advisor or researcher, or the project's own; default: the role's own).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-surface " "SURFACE"
local (default) or cloud: with cloud, resolve the registered base instance (<project>\-worker or \-reviewer) instead of a member; needs \-\-project.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-opt\-in
cloud only: confirm an opt\-in profile such as researcher.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
machine\-readable: role, class, profile, base, member, tier, model alias, model id, effort (cloud: instance, base, model, effort, profile, class).
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi role
List and check the roles: what a member or cloud agent is for.
.PP
A role is a directory with role.json (its class, write or read\-only, and a description) and ROLE.md (its instructions). Four ship with qargi: build, write, review and test (review is read\-only). Add your own under ~/.qargi/config/roles/NAME/: a name of one lower\-case token, never lead. The class decides what the guard allows a member (read\-only: nothing in the clone changes); the text of a role cannot change it. A task block carries the role's text; a cloud agent of the role has it in its system prompt.
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the roles, their class and where they live.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "show " "NAME"
Print a role: its class, description and instructions.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "prompt " "NAME"
Print a role's instructions as a task block carries them.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "check " "[NAME]"
Validate one role, or all of them.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi provider
List and check the model providers: tiers, models and what each can do.
.PP
A provider is a descriptor, lib/providers/NAME.json, with one schema for every provider: the model each tier (fast, balanced, deep, frontier) maps to, with the date and source it was verified, the effort levels it understands, the one surface it serves (local or cloud) and a capability matrix for it: model and effort overrides at dispatch, a fixed tool list per agent, a read\-only barrier, a session budget ceiling, memory stores, skills and MCP. A capability that is not verified counts as not supported. A provider with no read\-only barrier on its surface cannot serve the review role there unless you accept that with qargi provider accept. The project's models.provider chooses the provider; there is no default, and a verb that needs one refuses a project with none (exit 2). A family name (what several runtimes of one vendor share) stands for the runtime of a surface only in a project's models.provider and in provider of; every verb that takes a NAME (show, check, model, addenda, adapter\-run, effective, require\-barrier, layout, launch, require\-skills) takes a runtime name, and a family name is refused as an unknown provider.
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the providers with the surface each serves and whether the descriptor is clean.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "show " "NAME"
Print one provider: tiers and models, effort levels and its capability matrix.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
machine\-readable.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
show it as this project sees it, with its verified extensions composed in.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "accept " "NAME"
Record that you accept a provider surface that has no read\-only barrier.
.br
Without \-\-plan and \-\-approved it prints what would be recorded and a plan token. Applied, it writes models.accept_no_barrier.SURFACE (who, when, why) to the project's answers and the audit log. It is the only way a provider surface whose read\-only barrier is not supported can serve the review role.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-surface " "SURFACE"
the surface the acceptance covers.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-barrier
accept running the review role without a read\-only barrier on that surface.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project whose answers record it (default: the project of the current directory).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-asked " "WORDS"
your words asking for it.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your words approving the plan.
.RE
.RS
