.TP
.B qargi local
Prepare, verify, list and clean a local run that has no way to push.
.PP
The agent works in a clone of a bare copy that has no remote, runs git through a credential\-less wrapper, and verify compares every ref of the real repository before and after. It is a guard, not a sandbox.
.RS
.TP
.BI "run " "RUN"
Run a task through a command runtime (a vendor CLI) in a prepared run; \-\-dry\-run prints what would run.
.br
Only for a runtime whose dispatch mode is command; a runtime that works inside the session (dispatch mode in\-session) is refused: dispatch it as its notes say (qargi provider notes spawn). The command is built from the definition without a shell, runs in a scrubbed environment (only the names the pack's env_pass lists, which the review shows), and its output goes to OUT/transcript. A read\-only run needs the barrier the definition declares (or your acceptance). A library pack's executable runs only from its sealed copy. Exit status: 0 the command succeeded, 1 it failed (or a usage error, such as no task block), 2 refused (a barrier the run cannot have, a refused definition), 3 the runtime cannot be run this way (not a command runtime, its command is not on PATH), 4 transport (the definition maps the command's exit to it).
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-task " "FILE"
the file with the task block (default RUN/task.md).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-model " "MODEL"
a tier or a model id the runtime accepts (default the balanced tier).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-effort " "LEVEL"
an effort level; dropped with a note when the runtime cannot map it.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-timeout " "S"
seconds before the process group is killed (default from the definition).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-again
run a second time in the same run.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
print the argv, the working directory and the prompt, and run nothing.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B prepare
Make the clone and the wrapper for a local agent.
.br
Prints KEY=value lines: RUN, ROLE, CLASS, PROFILE (the role's own unless \-\-profile), MEMBER, MODEL and EFFORT (only with \-\-profile), CLONE, OUT, TMP, GIT, BASE_SHA and CONTRACT (the run's own copy of the member contract, with the contract addenda of the project's local runtime as runtime\-addenda.md). Then a blank line and a TASK\-BLOCK PATHS block (RUN_DIR, CLONE, OUT, TMP, GIT, BASE_SHA, CONTRACT) to copy into the task, and in a stored project a TASK\-BLOCK MEMORY block. A caller that evals the output must stop at the blank line.
When the project (found by matching the repository against the stored answers) has a render for its local runtime (packs apply \-\-target RUNTIME) that has drifted, prepare also prints a WARNING block to stderr with the findings and the fix, packs apply \-\-target RUNTIME PROJECT. The run goes on, and stdout and the TASK\-BLOCK PATHS block are unchanged.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-repo " "DIR"
the local repository.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-repo\-url " "URL"
clone a private repository from this https URL (no user name or password in it: that is \-\-credential\-file).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-credential\-file " "FILE"
token file for \-\-repo\-url.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-base " "BRANCH"
the base branch.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-slug " "SLUG"
the task slug (branch agent/<slug>).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-scratch " "DIR"
where to put the run.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-role " "ROLE"
the task's role, written to RUN/ability with its class in RUN/class; a read\-only role keeps the clone unchanged (default build).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " "NAME"
the profile to dispatch under (for example advisor or auditor for review); resolved before any clone, refused when its class does not fit the role, recorded in RUN/profile and printed with MEMBER, MODEL and EFFORT.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "verify " "RUN_DIR"
Compare the repository's refs with the snapshot taken by prepare.
.br
Exits 2 when refs changed (1 before), 3 when the lead's registry does not vouch for the run: it is not in it, names no repository for it, or RUN/repo.path, repo.url or cred.path differ from it (the repository and the credential come from the registry; a member can write the run directory). After a passing verify the run records RUN/verified, and later verify and clean compare with that: your own fast\-forwards of develop and master and of agent|feature|release|hotfix/* branches, and new branches there, are accepted; remote refs, tags, deletions and rewrites still fail. Run it on every run before merging any of them.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "clean " "[RUN_DIR]"
Remove a collected, verified run and its temporary directory.
.br
Prepare makes a private temporary directory for the run (TMP, on /dev/shm when it exists) that the agent puts its test sandboxes in; clean removes it with the clone, the bare copy and the output (only when it is a qargi\-run.XXXXXX directory under /dev/shm or QARGI_RUN_TMP, or inside the run). It refuses a run whose output has no manifest (collect first) and one whose repository refs changed (that is evidence). It also refuses a run whose clone holds commits that were never applied (review apply) or whose applied tip is on no branch but agent/* in the repository (feature merge first), so finished work is not deleted by mistake. \-\-discard is the deliberate way past all of these; it still refuses anything that prepare did not make. A run cleaned with \-\-applied needs a RUN/applied record and a repository path in the registry (a run of a repository URL is kept); \-\-applied takes no RUN_DIR and no \-\-discard. \-\-stale DAYS \-\-scratch DIR is a plan first: it prints each finished run older than DAYS days and changes nothing; with \-\-yes each is cleaned exactly as clean RUN would, so a stale run whose commits were never applied or merged is kept and named (exit status 1), never dropped. It is refused without \-\-scratch, takes no RUN_DIR, no \-\-applied and no \-\-discard, and \-\-yes is for \-\-stale only.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-discard
drop the run on purpose: also a run whose output holds no manifest, whose commits were never merged or whose repository refs changed (verify's outcome is printed as a warning).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-applied
clean every run under \-\-scratch whose work is recorded applied (review apply) and merged into a branch other than agent/*; the others are kept and named, and the exit status is 1 when an applied run was kept.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-stale " "DAYS"
list the finished runs (RUN/verified or RUN/applied exists) under \-\-scratch that are at least DAYS days old, from the run's start (a run with no start time is named as skipped); with \-\-yes clean each of them by the rules below, and keep and name those clean refuses; a run still in progress is never listed.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-yes
with \-\-stale: clean the runs it lists (without it nothing changes).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-scratch " "DIR"
with \-\-applied or \-\-stale: the directory the runs were prepared in; only the run directories directly under it are looked at.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the local runs: role, slug, age, whether applied and merged.
.br
Read\-only. One row per run: ROLE, SLUG, AGE, APPLIED (RUN/applied exists: review apply ran), MERGED (the applied tip is on a branch other than agent/*; ? for a run of a repository URL) and RUN. It is the list that qargi local clean \-\-applied \-\-scratch DIR acts on. A directory is a run only when the lead's registry names it (a directory a member makes beside its own run is not one), and the AGE is ? when the run's started file is not a number.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-scratch " "DIR"
the directory the runs were prepared in (default: the runs of the registry that still exist).
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi supervise
Find stalled agent runs: notify, prepare a nudge, never act alone.
.PP
Hooks only record (the run\-event verbs of qargi hook append ids, times, a tool name and a hash to a per\-run file under the state directory; no command, message or error text). One idempotent tick is the only actor: it folds those files, the local\-run registry and the session tracker into runs.json and classifies each unfinished run in a fixed order: waiting on an approval (not a stall), stopped by an API error, interim (turn ended, background work running, a cap of about 35 minutes), looping (three identical tool digests in a row), suspected dead (no activity for too long); for cloud sessions requires_action, budget_reached, stale while running, idle awaiting review. It acts only by printing `QARGI SUPERVISE <class> <run> <next\-action>` lines, appending to supervisor.log and the audit log (origin supervisor), and PREPARING a nudge in nudges.jsonl. A nudge is a fixed template, at most 2 per run with at least 10 minutes between them, never while the run waits on an approval or has little budget left, and never an approval. A script cannot message a local subagent: the coordinating session delivers the prepared nudge and records it with delivered, and only then does it count. A cloud nudge is sent only by nudge, which needs \-\-asked. Nothing sends automatically; the thresholds are data in lib/supervise\-defaults.json, conservative floors until `qargi report` shows real run lengths. Run the tick on a timer or a loop; status warns when it has not run for twice its interval.
.RS
.TP
.B tick
Fold the events, classify the runs, print what needs attention, prepare nudges.
.br
Run twice with nothing new it prints nothing the second time.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-now " "ISO"
act as of this time (2026\-10\-30T12:00:00Z); for tests and replays.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
print the result as JSON.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B status
Show the runs by class and the nudges waiting; warns when the tick is overdue.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-now " "ISO"
compare the last tick with this time.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
print the registry as JSON.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "nudge " "SID"
Send the fixed nudge to a cloud session, only when the caps allow.
.br
Refused when the session waits on an approval or has little budget left, when 2 nudges were already counted, or within 10 minutes of the last one. The message is the fixed template, never text from the agent.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-asked " "REQUEST"
the request, in your words, that names the cloud instance; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-now " "ISO"
act as of this time; for tests and replays.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "delivered " "KEY"
Record that you delivered a local run's prepared nudge.
.br
KEY is the agent id the tick printed. Only then does the nudge count against the cap.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-now " "ISO"
record this delivery time; for tests and replays.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B hooks
Print the settings.json hook snippet for the run\-event handlers.
.br
It never writes the file: merge the entries into your Claude Code settings.json yourself.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-qargi " "COMMAND"
the command to put in the snippet (default qargi).
.RE
.RE
.SS Review and apply
.TP
.B qargi review
Check what an agent returned and put it on a task branch.
.RS
.TP
.BI "check " "OUT"
Check what a run returned: numbered patches, messages, manifest, groups.
.br
OUT is the member's output directory (RUN_DIR/out); the patches are read from OUT/patches only, where review apply reads them. Refuses a monolithic patch, a commit without a message body and a manifest that does not match the patches. A review run passes with a report.md (or PREFIXreport.md, as in a team run) that has a Findings section instead of patches. Prints OK, WARN or FAIL lines and a summary; exit 1 when any line is FAIL (warnings alone exit 0).
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-empty
accept a manifest with no commits (a team member that was blocked or had nothing to change).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "apply " "RUN_DIR"
Apply a collected, checked run to a task branch of the repository.
.br
Refuses a run applied already, and unless review check passes for RUN_DIR/out (warnings allowed) and local verify accepts the run (a refs\-changed result prints the diff). Creates the branch at the commit the run's base branch had when the run was prepared and applies every patch of out/patches in order with git am, in a temporary worktree of the project's repository: your checkout is never touched and no other ref changes. The committer is the one the repository has configured, else the author of the patches. It refuses a branch that exists; a patch that does not apply removes the worktree and the branch and names the patch. On success it prints the commits and the next commands, and records branch and tip in RUN_DIR/applied and the branch in the run's ref snapshot, so local clean still accepts the run.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-branch " "BRANCH"
the branch to create (default agent/<slug> of the run).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B prune
List, or with \-\-yes delete, the agent/* branches already merged into develop or a feature.
.br
A branch is merged when it is an ancestor of develop or of any feature/* branch (the test git branch \-d makes, against that target rather than the checked\-out branch). Only local agent/* branches are considered; a branch checked out in a worktree is kept; no other branch is touched. Prints each branch it lists or deleted.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-yes
delete them (without it nothing changes).
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi team
Run a task with a lead and role agents (opt\-in).
.PP
For a change that affects usage: the build member changes the code, the write member updates the operational docs, the review member judges both. The lead is this Claude Code session, a cloud lead, or a session started in the console; all follow the lead contract. Each member returns its own series against its own base.
.RS
.TP
.BI "brief " "[PROJECT] TEAM"
Print the brief a lead needs for one task.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-task " "TEXT"
what to do.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-done " "COMMAND"
an executable check for the builder's part.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-base " "BRANCH"
the builder's base branch (needed when the project says @dispatcher).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-write\-done " "COMMAND"
the writer's check.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-review\-done " "COMMAND"
the reviewer's check.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "check " "RUN_DIR"
Check a collected team run: every member and the lead's report.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B contract
Print the lead's contract.
.br
The procedure every lead follows, whoever it is.
.RE
.SS Memory
.TP
.B qargi memory
Check a memory store, list its tiers, read a note, split, merge or copy stores.
.PP
split, merge and copy work between two stores, each a directory of notes, a .jsonl dump (read\-only: a source, never a target) or a memstore_ id. A note with a name starting with a dot, or that a store cannot hold, makes the plan refuse and name it. Nothing is deleted: what a split moves out of a store goes to /_archive/<date>/ and a collision to /_conflicts/<date>/, and the origin of every note written is added to its Sources: line, so each change can be undone. Without \-\-approved they print the plan and its token and change nothing; a store whose notes changed since the plan is refused.
.RS
.TP
.BI "lint " "STORE_ID"
Check a store against the canon.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-kind " "KIND"
recipes or reference (for a store before the canon).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "get " "STORE_ID PATH"
Print one note.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B tiers
List the project's memory tiers (repo, cloud, project, shared, system): where each is, whether it exists, its notes.
.br
A tier is one memory store of the standard layout at a fixed rank, highest first: repo > cloud > project > shared > system. Where they are comes from the project's answers (memory.tiers; each key is optional and has a default). The reference store is not a tier. Reads only; nothing is created or changed.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
machine\-readable.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B check
Check every existing memory tier against the canon, and that no note lives in two tiers.
.br
Runs the canon check on each tier that exists, then reports a note path present in two or more tiers (structural files such as README.md and INDEX.md repeat per tier and are fine). A tier that does not exist (memory init creates it) or cannot be reached is information, not a failure. Exit 1 when anything failed.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "init " "TIER"
Create a memory tier (repo, project, shared or system) as an empty store of the standard layout.
.br
Without \-\-approved it shows what would be created and a plan token and changes nothing. It refuses a tier that holds files that are not a standard store, one whose place overlaps another tier, a repo tier outside the checkout, and the cloud tier (provision creates that). The repo tier is written inside your repository checkout: qargi never commits it, so review it and commit or ignore it yourself. A tier that is already set up says so and exits 0.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "move " "PATH"
Move notes from one tier to another, with the original archived in the first.
.br
PATH is one note (ending in .md) or a folder prefix under shared/ or roles/ROLE/. Only notes move, never indexes. A note must live in one tier, so it refuses a path that is also in another tier. Nothing is deleted: the original goes to /_archive/<date>/ of the first tier and each copy records its origin. Without \-\-approved it prints the plan and its token and changes nothing; a tier whose notes changed since the plan is refused.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-from " "TIER"
the tier the notes are in.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-to " "TIER"
the tier they go to.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "split " "FROM TO"
Copy the notes a selector picks into another store, optionally archiving the originals.
.br
At least one of \-\-prefix, \-\-role and \-\-note\-kind is needed; a note must match every one given. Notes already in TO are left; a different one goes to /_conflicts/<date>/ in TO.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-prefix " "PATH"
pick the notes under this path (repeat for several).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-role " "ROLE"
pick the notes under /roles/ROLE/.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-note\-kind " "KIND"
pick the notes whose Kind: header is KIND (setup, gate, rule, ...).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-archive
move the originals to /_archive/<date>/ in FROM (with their origin in Sources:).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "merge " "FROM TO"
Add every note of FROM that TO lacks.
.br
FROM is only read. A note whose original sits in TO's archive is moved back from it, so merging what a split wrote undoes the split.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-policy " "POLICY"
what to do when a note differs: conflicts (put the incoming one in /_conflicts/<date>/, default) or keep (leave it and report).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "copy " "FROM TO"
Copy notes into another store, leaving the source as it is.
.br
Without a selector every note is copied except /CANON.md. Each copy records its origin in Sources:.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-prefix " "PATH"
pick the notes under this path (repeat for several).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-role " "ROLE"
pick the notes under /roles/ROLE/.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-note\-kind " "KIND"
pick the notes whose Kind: header is KIND (setup, gate, rule, ...).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-policy " "POLICY"
what to do when a note differs: conflicts (put the incoming one in /_conflicts/<date>/, default) or keep (leave it and report).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
